# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx remote boot [arch] [--kernel file] [--bootfs file] [--image name]
               [-k nodekey] [--key file] node [option ...]
vx remote trace [arch] [-k nodekey] [--key file] [--out dir]
                [--kernel file] [--bootfs file] node seconds [word ...]
vx remote test [arch] --serial dev [--timeout seconds] [--option word]...
               [--record] [-k nodekey] [--key file] platform node
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx remote boot` warm-boots a node from the dev machine, in one step: it opens a cpu session (vx-cpu(1)) to <node>, runs warmboot(8) there on a kernel and bootfs from this machine, and waits until the new boot's cpu server answers.
Without `--kernel` and `--bootfs` it first builds <arch> (`amd64` if none is given) and its cpu image, `build/<arch>/bootfs-cpu.img`, as `vx boot --cpu` would (vx-build(1)), with this terminal's key and vx's node key, and sends `build/<arch>/kernel/kernel.elf` and that image.
With `--image` it builds and sends `build/<arch>/bootfs-`<name>`.img` instead: `board-cpu` is a board's, every service with a cpu server and fs on its NVMe disk, and `board-desktop` the same with the desktop (board-test(8)).
The node sees the two files, and nothing else of this machine's, at `/mnt/term/root/kernel.elf` and `/mnt/term/root/bootfs.img`, links to them in a directory of their own under `$TMPDIR`, removed afterwards.
With both given it runs anywhere; to build, it runs from the repository root.
The <option>s are the new boot's command line; without any it keeps the node's.

warmboot copies the images, prints `warmboot: handing over` and asks devmgr for the warm boot (reboot(8)).
Once the node has read the bootfs to its end `vx remote boot` dials it again every 200 ms, at most four dials at once, each given 1 s; if the kernel refuses, warmboot prints `warmboot: refused` after the reason, and `vx remote boot` ends.
A node that answers is the new boot when its `/dev/warm/kernel` is empty, as only a fresh boot's is.
Both boots answer with the node key vx built in (cpu(8)), so `-k` is needed only for an image with another; `--key` names this terminal's key file, as for vx-cpu(1).

It prints how long the copies took and how long after them the new boot's cpu server answered, with that as a budget line, `budget name=remote.warmboot` in nanoseconds, against 2 s, or for a board's image (`--image board-*`) `budget name=remote.warmboot.board`, against 5 s.

`vx remote trace` profiles a node in one step: a cpu session runs trace(8) there for <seconds>, the <word>s its start command's (`hz <n>`, `pages <n>`, categories), copying the recording to <dir> (`build/vx/trace`, emptied first), which the node sees as `/mnt/term/root`; then it prints the profile as `vx trace profile` (vx-trace(1)) would, from <arch>'s build or the `--kernel` and `--bootfs` given.
It builds nothing: the node should be running that build.

`vx remote test` runs the native suite on a board: what boot-smoke runs in QEMU, the kernel's self-tests, m2 and every Swift suite.
It builds <arch>, then warm-boots <node> as `vx remote boot` does into `build/<arch>/kernel/kernel.elf` and `build/<arch>/bootfs.img`, boot-smoke's image, with `userboot.next=bin/m2 croi.test=all userboot.shutdown=reboot croi.halt_on_panic=false` and each `--option`'s <word> (`croi.console=direct`, say: debuglog(2)); the new boot has no cpu server, so it doesn't wait for one.
It reads the board's console from the serial port <dev> at 115200 baud, opened before the warm boot, into `build/vx/remote-test-`<platform>`.log`, as qemu.sh reads QEMU's (vx-test(1)): the run passes at `userboot: bin/m2 exited with 0` after the new kernel's banner, and fails at a panic, another exit, or nothing on the console for <seconds> (900 by default).
It then lists every budget line the run printed, marking those over their limits, which it reports and doesn't judge (vx-bench(1) judges budgets).
The board reboots when the run ends, into what its firmware boots by default, or after a panic, as `croi.halt_on_panic=false` asks.
With `--record` it writes the run to `docs/hardware/runs.tsv` (D66): the date, this tree's commit, which must be committed, <platform> (an id in `docs/hardware/support.tsv`), `pass` or `fail`, and `native` when it passed; vx-hardware(1) takes a platform's `last_pass` from these.

# EXAMPLES

Boot amd64 with a cpu server, then, from another terminal, rebuild and hand the node to the new build:

```rc
% vx boot --cpu
```

```rc
% vx remote boot localhost
vx remote boot: localhost into kernel.elf and bootfs-cpu.img
warmboot: handing over
vx remote boot: 108.6 MB copied in 0.89 s (121.9 MB/s)
vx remote boot: back on cpu 1.63 s after the copies
  budget name=remote.warmboot value=1630264000 unit=ns limit=2000000000
```

Run the native suite on the Orange Pi 6 Plus, running a cpu image, its console on this machine's USB serial adapter, and record it:

```rc
% sg dialout -c 'vx remote test arm64 --serial /dev/ttyUSB0 \
    --record cix-sky1 192.168.0.19'
```

Profile the node for two seconds:

```rc
% vx remote trace localhost 2
```

